Market Drivers
Supply Chain Digitalization — Companies are adopting connected packaging technologies to improve product visibility, inventory management, traceability, and logistics efficiency.
Growing Anti-Counterfeiting Requirements — Digital authentication technologies help brands verify products and strengthen protection against counterfeit goods.
Expansion of E-Commerce — Increasing online purchasing is creating demand for packaging solutions that provide tracking, authentication, and interactive consumer experiences.

Vertical Industry Watch
IoT-enabled packaging is evolving from conventional product containment toward connected systems that generate and exchange product information throughout the supply chain. RFID and NFC technologies support identification and tracking, while QR codes enable consumers to access digital information through smartphones. Smart sensors can monitor environmental conditions such as temperature, humidity, and product integrity, which is particularly important for pharmaceuticals, food, beverages, and other sensitive products. Connected packaging also supports brand authentication and engagement by providing customers with information about product origin, usage, and authenticity. Manufacturers are increasingly integrating IoT platforms with supply-chain management systems to improve visibility and operational efficiency. As connected-device costs decline and digital infrastructure expands, IoT-enabled packaging is gaining relevance across multiple industries.

